h  the ability to detect patterns make  decisions and predict outcomes using IOT  data and running on that big data  infrastructure so as we can see we have  the data coming from the Internet of  Things into the big data architecture  being processed by the machine learning  algorithms but closing the loop  essentially means allowing the machine  learning algorithms to execute commands  back down on those devices in the  Internet of Things this allows the  artificial intelligence systems to  affect the world around them then they  sense the results of their actions to  see whether they're producing positive  or negative results and then feed that  data back through their models to learn  from their results and optimize their  behavior over time closing the loop  completely eliminates the human being in  the middle and leads to fully autonomous  intelligent systems capable of making  rational decisions in response to their  environment and learning how to optimize  their behavior over time over the next  few decades we're going to see the  emergence of these fully autonomous  intelligent systems also known as smart  systems cloud robotics cyber-physical  systems or  artificial intelligence for example will  have self-driving cars that will  eventually replace all human drivers in  automobiles semis boats and airplanes  will also have general-purpose robots  that will be able to perform manual  labor without being explicitly  programmed to do so like this robot  Baxter here you put it in training mode  you essentially show it what to do by  moving its arms around and it  essentially learns how to do that  without a person typing move your hand  left by two inch or two centimeters and  then turn thirty degrees and then like  we saw with that tipsy robot bartender  earlier we'll also have smart buildings  that can optimize their own energy  efficiency and anticipate the needs of  their users I've worked with several  clients building exactly these types of  solutions in the intelligent lighting  industry and will eventually have smart  cities that are capable of optimizing  municipal resources like electricity  water waste and traffic without any  human intervention will embed artificial  intelligence in all aspects of our  environment essentially computers will  become invisible as artificial  intelligence will just be woven into the  fabric of our society and now this is  gonna have a significant impact on our  economy and capitalism as we know it  first this will likely lead to a  significantly more nonlinear economy  those with these smart machines are  gonna have even more power and those  without smart machines unfortunately are  going to have even less power in  addition that is likely going to become  one of the most valuable resources in  the information economy those that have  the most data and the ability to power  AI with it are going to wield tremendous  power in the new information economy  this is also gonna have an enormous  impact on our labour economy as well  AI will eventually replace all human  labor at least the jobs we don't want to  do at some point time in the future  given the economics driving this trend  it's not a matter of if a job is going  to be replaced by AI it's more a matter  of when it's going to be replaced in  fact some experts are currently  attempting to predict which jobs will be  most likely automated or eliminated  based on the repetitiveness and  complexity in fact we can even determine  which technologies will be necessary in  order to eliminate certain jobs for  example we can see which retail jobs  will likely to be eliminated in the  coming years as AI continues to be  applied to the world of retail sales  even the medical industry is an immune  to this coming wave of automation while  these medical tasks are generally more  complex and less repetitive than the  retail tasks we saw they're rapidly  becoming within reach of modern AI then  we can extrapolate this information to  det