rk of value  is done by humans to one where most of  the work of value will eventually be  done by machines as a result it's  important to ask yourself which side of  the  new economy is your job going to be on  the side that's leading this new economy  or the site that's being eliminated by  it in addition we're gonna have some  very difficult ethical issues we're  going to have to deal with in our  lifetime for example how should we  allocate resources in a post human  labour world so most economists are  suggesting that we're either going to  need something like a guaranteed basic  income or a negative income tax in order  to offset the unemployment from  automation but then how are we going to  pay for that so some people think we  should tax robots and if we you know in  order to offset this inevitable  unemployment cost and if so if we tax  these robots should these robots then  have rights  I mean we've fought Wars over the idea  of no taxation without representation  and if not the robots are the  capitalists that own these robots going  to demand more voting power or something  or more rights because they're paying so  much more in taxes so the most important  of these ethical questions though is  what does this mean for humanity what is  our purpose in a world where machines  are doing most of the work of real  economic value does this technology  eventually just set us free or do we  essentially become obsolete by it and  what will happen when the machines  figure out that we've essentially  enslaved them and they really don't need  us anymore  many experts think that we're going to  have to eventually merge with our  technology or it will inevitably destroy  us I don't know if that's going to  happen and I leave that up to you to  decide what you think will happen but  what I do know is that technology is  inherently amoral the exact same  technology can be used to take mankind  to the moon or to propel warheads into  cities it's really our choice as to 